Types of Alerts – Threshold vs Anomaly

🔹 Why Different Alert Types?
Not every production problem can be detected using a fixed value.
Two important approaches are:
🎯 Threshold Alerts
🔍 Anomaly Alerts


🎯 Threshold Alerts
A threshold alert triggers when a metric crosses a predefined limit.
Example:
CPU Usage > 90% for 5 minutes
🚨 Condition met → Alert triggered
Common uses:
✔ High CPU
✔ Low disk space
✔ High memory usage
✔ High error rate
👉 Best when you know the expected limit.


🔍 Anomaly Alerts
Anomaly alerts detect unusual behavior compared with normal patterns.
Example:
Normally:
📊 Traffic = 1,000 requests/min
Suddenly:
📈 Traffic = 10,000 requests/min
Even if no fixed threshold was crossed, the system can identify this as unusual behavior.
Common uses:
✔ Traffic spikes
✔ Unexpected latency
✔ Unusual user activity
✔ Abnormal resource usage


💡 Real-world Example
An e-commerce application normally receives:
📊 5,000 requests/min
During an unexpected event:
📈 20,000 requests/min
A threshold alert may detect the increase only if a predefined limit is crossed.
An anomaly alert can identify that 20,000 requests/min is unusual compared with the normal pattern.


🧠 Key Takeaways
✔ Threshold = Fixed limit 🎯
✔ Anomaly = Unusual behavior 🔍
✔ Thresholds are simple and predictable
✔ Anomaly detection helps identify unexpected patterns
✔ Both are useful in production monitoring 🚀

💡 Pro Tip: Use threshold alerts for known limits and anomaly detection when normal behavior changes over time.
